# tasty-bench output. The `bench` CI job writes both of these to the repo root,
# and comparing runs locally tends to leave more of them around. The one CSV
# that is meant to be tracked lives at bench/baseline-*.csv and is named for
# the GHC and architecture that produced it -- see bench/README.md.
/bench-results.csv
/bench-baseline.csv

# Editor
.vscode/

# Claude Code session state. The worktrees directory is per-session scratch and
# must never be committed; settings.json and the hooks/ scripts it points at
# are deliberately shared and stay tracked.
#
# '.claude/*' rather than '.claude/': git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so under '.claude/' the negations below silently do nothing and
# even 'git add .claude/settings.json' is refused. Matching the contents
# instead leaves the directory traversable, which is what makes the negations
# work. The same applies one level down: '.claude/hooks' must be un-excluded
# before '.claude/hooks/*' can be.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/hooks
.claude/hooks/*
!.claude/hooks/*.sh

# Symlink into the nix store that git-hooks.nix writes on `nix develop` (see
# flake.nix's `preCommit`). Generated from flake.nix -- not a source file.
/.pre-commit-config.yaml

# sokoban-shot writes a PPM of the game's own window, and defaults to writing
# it into whatever directory it was run from. They are 7MB each and are working
# output, not evidence to keep: capture/ppm2png.py turns one into something to
# look at, and the shot can always be retaken.
*.ppm
repomix-output.xml
